<card id="c" title="How a Browser Renders a Web Page">
<do type="prev" label="Back"><prev/></do>
<p><a href="https://developers.google.com/web/fundamentals/performance/critical-rendering-path/render-tree-construction">The render tree</a> represents the combination of the DOM and CSSOM, and what will be rendered on the page. It doesn&apos;t necessarily mean that all nodes in the render tree will be visually present, for example, nodes with styles like opacity: 0 or visibility: hidden will be included and can still be read by screen readers etc., whereas those set to display: none will not be included. Additionally, tags like &lt;head&gt; that don&apos;t contain visual information will always be skipped.</p>
<p>Different browsers have different <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_browser_engines">rendering engines</a>, just as is the case with JavaScript engines.</p>
